CONTEXT (from the previous page). Gauss is analysing the telescope of his Repsold meridian circle. The objective has focal length a = 2188 mm. Each of three eyepieces consists of two lenses. On f.10 they are listed as 'first glass', focal length c, and 'second glass', focal length b, with distances from the front rim of the eyepiece tube. Their separation is called q. Note that q is NOT a magnification despite the label 'Vergrösserung q': 23.80-4.15 = 19.65, 20.70-3.45 = 17.25, 26.00-5.20 = 20.80. The numbers 95.77, 91.63, 77.46 are the magnifications m = a(q-c-b)/(bc) (Gauss's sign gives m < 0, an inverted image). Check: 2188*(-43.18)/(30.77*32.06) = -95.77, and likewise 91.6 and 77.46. The ray-tracing on this page shows that, in light order, the lens nearer the objective is b (field lens) and the eye lens is c. So the 'first glass' (c) is the one nearer the eye-end rim, and '2tes Ocular' means the second lens met by the light, i.e. the eye lens.

1. PARAXIAL TRANSFER COEFFICIENTS. Let s = b+c-q. Gauss tabulates, at three stations, two independent solutions of the paraxial ray equations:
- station 0: the objective;
- station ': the field lens b, at distance a + b(c-q)/s behind the objective;
- station '': the eye lens c, a further q behind.

The α,β column is the axis-parallel ray entering the objective at unit height:
- α = 1, β = 1/a;
- α' = b(c-q)/(a s), β' = -b/(a s);
- α'' = -bc/(a s), β'' = 0. The ray leaves parallel, so the system is afocal (telescope focused for infinity).

The γ,δ column is the chief ray through the centre of the objective with unit inclination:
- γ = 0, δ = 1;
- γ' = a + b(c-q)/s, the distance objective to field lens;
- δ' = a/b - b/s;
- γ'' = -a(b-q)/b - bc/s;
- δ'' = -a s/(bc) = m, the angular magnification.

IMPLICIT (mine). The tacit condition is that the objective's focus coincides with the front focal point of the eyepiece. That point lies b(c-q)/s in front of lens b, which is the front focal distance f(1-q/c) with eyepiece focal length f = bc/s. Re-tracing gives the same magnitudes. The signs agree with Gauss's up to a fixed convention: at stations 0 and '' the off-diagonal entries are reversed (angle sign reversed); at station ' the diagonal entries are reversed. In every case αδ - βγ = 1 at each station, the Gaussian unimodularity (determinant 1) of the transfer.

2. EYE DISTANCE (exit pupil). The eye belongs where the chief ray recrosses the axis behind the eye lens: -γ''/δ''. This equals c(b-q)/s + b²c²/(a s²). That is the back focal distance f(1-q/b) of the eyepiece plus the Newtonian term f²/a, the image of the objective (which sits at x = a before the front focus) formed at x' = f²/a. Since m² = a²s²/(b²c²), this is c(b-q)/s + a/m², as written. A cancelled first attempt, c(q-b)/(q-b-c), is the same first term.

Numerically:
- eyepiece 1: 8.26 + 0.24 = 8.5 mm;
- eyepiece 2: 14.68 + 0.26 = 14.9 mm;
- eyepiece 3: 14.14 + 0.36 = 14.5 mm.

All agree with the page. The second column (4.3, 11.4, 9.3) equals eye distance minus the distance of the eye lens from the front rim of the tube (8.5-4.15, 14.9-3.45, 14.5-5.20). It is therefore the required position of the eye beyond the tube rim; the reading 'zu verlangen' is uncertain.

3. FIELD OF VIEW. The field is limited by the field-lens stop, giving a half-field θ = (radius)/γ'. Full fields:
- eyepiece 1: γ' = 2196.8 and radius 9.2 (from 18.40) give 2θ = 28.8', i.e. 29';
- eyepiece 2: γ' = 2198.8 and radius 6.5 (from 13.00) give 20.3', i.e. 20';
- eyepiece 3: γ' = 2202.9 and radius 11 give 34.3', i.e. 34'.

This matches Gauss's proviso 'if eyepiece 3 has an 11 mm opening' only if 11 mm is the semi-aperture (diameter 22.00, as listed on f.10). With 11 mm as the diameter one would get only 17'.

4. BOTTOM. A separate, non-mathematical note gives pairs of birth (geb.) and death (†) years of husbands ('er') and wives ('sie'), plus a marriage ('Heir.'):
- Hauensch[ild?]: 1757–1808 and 1770–1818;
- Reinhold: 1767–1819 … 1822;
- Gräffe: 1754–1816 and 1760–1826. This is probably J. F. C. Gräffe, the Göttingen theologian, d. 1816.

The data look like records of the Göttingen professors' widows' fund, which Gauss analysed (conjecture).
